# 6. Dynamic Expert Spawning and Bottom-Up Routing

## 6.1 The Failure of Fixed-Capacity and Flat Architectures
Flat MARL architectures collapse at the Sparsity Wall due to temporal discount dilution ($ \gamma^{250} \approx 0.08 $). Causal Affordance Routing (CAR) and Causal Advantage Routing (CIR) fail. Monolithic architectures lack temporal abstraction and modular capacity. Traditional H-MARL approaches like ROMA or MAHIRO introduce this abstraction. They rely on fixed-capacity networks or top-down manager-worker structures. They are prone to non-stationarity and goal collapse.

We introduce a decentralized model. It is a Dynamic Skill-Pool Co-op architecture. We refer to it as CO-OP (Confidence-Oriented Option Pool).

## 6.2 The CO-OP Architecture

CO-OP decouples the physical agent from the policy. The architecture consists of a dynamically expanding pool of Expert Neural Networks $ \mathcal{E} = \{E_1, E_2, \dots, E_K\} $.

Any agent $ i $ can route its execution to any Expert in the pool. The agent's identity vector $ e_{\text{role}_i} $ is concatenated with the local observation $ o_i $ before being processed by the Expert.

### 6.2.1 Bottom-Up Confidence Routing
CO-OP employs a bottom-up voting mechanism. It replaces a top-down manager. 

At step $ t $, the state $ s_t $ is passed to all $ K $ currently active Experts. Each Expert $ E_k $ outputs an action distribution $ \pi_k(a|s_t) $ and a Value prediction $ V_k(s_t) $. The Value prediction is the Expert's Confidence Score. The global router selects the Expert with the highest confidence:
$$$
k^* = \arg\max_{k \in \{1 \dots K\}} V_k(s_t)
$$$
The agent executes the action sampled from $ \pi_{k^*} $. Causal Affordance Credit (CAR) is routed to the winning Expert. 

CO-OP eliminates manual reward shaping by implementing structural affordance detection. It detects an affordance by evaluating the team's aggregate action-mask volume. If $ \sum \text{mask}_{t+1} > \sum \text{mask}_t $ and an agent executed the `INTERACT` action, that Expert is causally credited for expanding the team's physical capabilities.

### 6.2.2 Binary Success Masking Integration
CO-OP integrates MARC's binary success masking and exponential alarm weighting. 

The base rewards for the active experts are modulated by a macro weighting factor $ \Omega_t $:
$$$
\Omega_t = \begin{cases} 
\exp\left(-\alpha \frac{A_t}{A_{max}}\right) & \text{if Expert } k \text{ triggered an affordance} \\
O \cdot \exp\left(-\alpha \frac{A_t}{A_{max}}\right) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
$$$
Here, $ O \in \{1.0, 0.5\} $ is the binary terminal outcome of the episode (using 0.5 to half the negative penalty on unshielded failing trajectories), and $ A_t $ is the global alarm level, normalized by $ A_{max} $. CO-OP multiplies the immediate rewards by $ \Omega_t $ before calculating the Generalized Advantage Estimate. It shields competent upstream experts from downstream incompetence. The decentralized router accurately assesses confidence.

### 6.2.3 Dynamic Capacity Evolution (Spawning and Pruning)
CO-OP dynamically adjusts the size of the Expert pool $ K $ through reactive value-threshold spawning. 

If an agent encounters a novel state space and the maximum confidence across all experts drops below a critical threshold $ \tau_{spawn} $:
$$$
\max(V_1(s_t), \dots, V_K(s_t)) < \tau_{spawn}
$$$
A new Expert $ E_{K+1} $ is appended to the pool. Spawning strictly requires all other experts to fall below $ \tau_{spawn} $. 

Instead of random initialization, CO-OP uses weight cloning. The new Expert $ E_{K+1} $ is initialized as an exact parameter clone of the parent Expert $ E_K $. We inject standard Gaussian noise ($ \sigma = 0.05 $) into the cloned weights. This retains foundational movement and locomotion priors while forcing mechanical divergence in familiar state distributions. If an Expert $ E_k $ is not selected by any agent for a continuous window of $ W $ episodes, it is pruned from the pool.

## 6.3 Evolutionary Confidence-Oriented Option Pool (E-COOP)
Reactive spawning in CO-OP introduces catastrophic failure modes. Value functions in reinforcement learning scale dynamically with the episodic return. Hardcoding a static threshold $ \tau_{spawn} $ guarantees structural collapse across multi-stage curricula. Furthermore, uniform Gaussian noise destroys tightly correlated neural pathways.

We introduce Evolutionary CO-OP (E-COOP). E-COOP replaces reactive spawning with a rigid generational cadence. E-COOP triggers an evolutionary crossover event at step 500, followed by subsequent crossovers every 200 updates. This cadence decouples expert generation from raw value magnitude.

### 6.3.1 FIM-Scaled Asexual Mutation
E-COOP abandons uniform noise injection. We compute the empirical Fisher Information Matrix (FIM) for the best-performing parent expert across a sampled microbatch. The FIM $ F_i $ quantifies the curvature of the log-likelihood space for parameter $ i $.

We mutate the cloned child expert using inverse square-root FIM scaling:
$$$
\theta_{i}^{(child)} = \theta_{i}^{(parent)} + \mathcal{N}\left(0, \frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{F_i}}\right)
$$$
This scaling physically forces the mutation to respect the Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) trust region. Parameters with high confidence (large $ F_i $) receive negligible noise. Parameters with flat gradients receive exploratory perturbations.

### 6.3.2 Optimizer Cold Starts and Grace Periods
Inheriting the Adam optimizer momentum tensors ($ m_t, v_t $) after a physical weight translation causes uncalibrated ballistic jumps. E-COOP deletes the Adam state for the child expert immediately after mutation. The optimizer executes a mathematical cold start. 

To prevent premature algorithmic pruning during this cold start phase, E-COOP enforces a routing Grace Period. The global router overrides the standard value competition and forces the selection of the child expert for 25 epochs. The child establishes baseline competence before competing against the parent.

### 6.3.3 Hybrid Routing Hysteresis
At value zero-crossings, standard CO-OP suffers from routing chatter. Two experts with nearly identical value predictions trade control at every timestep. This fragments the recurrent state.

E-COOP implements a Hybrid Routing Hysteresis mechanism. The router switches from the current expert to a rival expert only if the rival's value exceeds a compound threshold:
$$$
V_{rival} > V_{current} + \max(\epsilon_{abs}, \epsilon_{rel} \cdot |V_{current}|)
$$$
This mechanism provides a static absolute margin $ \epsilon_{abs} $ for value regions near zero, but transitions to a relative margin $ \epsilon_{rel} $ for large scale returns. This prevents chatter without requiring complex variance normalization.
